\verb{logs/original-package/}. A file from an archive in the folder
that is identical to a file next to it, and a README identical to
another, are left out.

R and Python scripts (\code{.R}, \code{.Rmd}, \code{.qmd}, \code{.py}, \code{.ipynb}) are changed so
they still find their files in the new layout, run from the top folder:
each file path that \code{\link[=repro_rewrite_paths]{repro_rewrite_paths()}} can match to one file of the
folder is replaced by that file's new path, as is the path a script writes
a file to that is in the folder or goes to \verb{outputs/}; and each
\code{setwd()}/\code{os.chdir()} line that sets a folder written out in the script,
no folder, or the script's own folder is replaced by a statement that does
nothing, with the original line kept in a comment (one that sets a folder
worked out when the code runs, such as \code{setwd(tempdir())}, is left). Every
change is reported, and listed in \code{CHANGES.md}. Scripts in other
languages (Stata, SPSS, MATLAB, ...) are copied unchanged, so their file
paths may no longer work.

Code that \code{code_check} recovers from an output file (a rendered R Markdown
or Quarto page, an SPSS \code{.spv}, a Stata \code{.smcl} or an Mplus \code{.out} file)
is kept as a script in \verb{analysis/}, unless the folder has the script it was
made from. In a README that the folder has, a link to a file of the folder
(such as an image) is changed to that file's new place.

Metadata is written next to the files:
\itemize{
\item \code{dataset_description.json} in each study folder with data (Psych-DS), describing
every data column, with its label from the codebook when
\code{codebook_check} found one;
\item \code{ro-crate-metadata.json} at the top (RO-Crate 1.3): every file, the
paper, the authors with their CRediT roles when the paper states them,
the contact, funding (the paper's funding statement, or what
\code{funding_check} finds in its text), the licence, the languages and
packages the code uses, the software proprietary formats need, which
files each script reads and writes, the missing-value codes, and how each
study was run (DDI-Lifecycle terms). "unknown" is written only where the
authors are asked for something: the licence, an author's CRediT roles,
and when and how the data were collected and processed;
\item \code{README.md}, a readable version of the RO-Crate, in the top folder and in
each study folder, unless the folder has its own README there. Without a
paper, the title is taken from the folder's own README;
\item \code{requirements.txt}, the Python packages the code imports, for
\verb{pip install -r requirements.txt}, unless the folder has its own
\code{requirements.txt}, \code{environment.yml} or \code{pyproject.toml}. R packages are
listed in the README and the RO-Crate;
\item \verb{logs/}, the list of files and the results of the checks, without the
places on this computer the files are in;
\item \code{CHANGES.md}, and a \code{CHANGES.md} in each study folder that has none.
